      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Triangular Factor of a QR Factorization Given the Orthogonal Factor",
      "kp_id": "e-8324"
    },
    "q-95931": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95931",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
