      "source_example_id": "e-8325",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns",
      "kp_id": "e-8325"
    },
    "q-96015": {
      "dom_id": "step-q96015",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{Q}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{21}\\cdot {r}_{11}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
